Article 1: Biologically informed deep neural network for prostate cancer discovery by Elmarakeby et al., published in Nature in 2021.

Objective: The researchers developed P-NET, a biologically informed deep-learning model to classify prostate cancer disease states and identify genomic alterations associated with advanced, treatment-resistant disease.

Dataset and inputs: The study included 1,013 prostate cancer cases (680 primary cancers and 333 castration-resistant prostate cancers). The model used somatic mutations and copy-number alterations (CNA).

Methods: P-NET was trained using an 80% training, 10% validation and 10% testing split. Its performance was compared with conventional machine-learning models, including logistic regression, support vector machines and decision trees.

Main findings: P-NET achieved an AUC of 0.93, AUPRC of 0.88 and accuracy of 0.83 on the test set. The model also identified important genomic alterations and pathways associated with advanced disease. CNA features were more informative than mutations in this particular analysis.

Relevance to our project: This paper supports the use of somatic mutations and CNA for prostate cancer classification. It also provides an example of model comparison, external validation and model interpretability.

Important difference: The paper classified primary versus metastatic/castration-resistant disease, whereas our proposed project aims to predict Stage 1–3 versus Stage 4. Therefore, our target and dataset need to be evaluated separately.

Reference: Elmarakeby HA et al. Biologically informed deep neural network for prostate cancer discovery. Nature. 2021;598:348–352. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-03922-4



Article 2: A DNA Copy Number Alteration Classifier as a Prognostic Tool for Prostate Cancer Patients

Authors: Ebrahimizadeh et al.
Journal: British Journal of Cancer, 2023
Link: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41416-023-02236-8

Objective

The researchers aimed to develop a CNA-based classifier to predict biochemical recurrence in prostate cancer patients. Biochemical recurrence means that cancer-related indicators, such as PSA levels, rise again after treatment.

Dataset and Inputs

The study examined 448 prostate cancer tumours from patients with low- or intermediate-risk disease. The researchers assessed copy number alterations in 14 genes using a laboratory method called multiplex ligation-dependent probe amplification (MLPA).

Methods

The researchers used Random Survival Forest to identify informative CNA features and Cox proportional hazards regression to develop a six-gene classifier. They also tested the classifier using data from other patient cohorts.

Main Findings

The six-gene CNA classifier was associated with biochemical recurrence and provided additional prognostic information beyond standard clinical factors. Its performance was also evaluated in independent datasets.

Relevance to Our Project

This paper demonstrates that CNA features can contain useful information for predicting prostate cancer outcomes. It supports our plan to evaluate a CNA-only approach and compare it with somatic mutation and combined mutation+CNA approaches.

Limitation

The study predicted biochemical recurrence, not cancer stage. Therefore, its prediction target differs from our proposed classification of Stage 1–3 versus Stage 4.

Reference

Ebrahimizadeh W, et al. A DNA copy number alteration classifier as a prognostic tool for prostate cancer patients. British Journal of Cancer. 2023;128:2165–2174. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41416-023-02236-8



Article 3: A Machine Learning Framework Develops a DNA Replication Stress Model for Predicting Clinical Outcomes and Therapeutic Vulnerability in Primary Prostate Cancer

Authors: Huang et al.
Journal: Journal of Translational Medicine, 2023
Link: https://doi.org/10.1186/s12967-023-03872-7

Objective: The researchers aimed to develop a machine-learning model to predict prostate cancer recurrence and identify possible treatment vulnerabilities using a DNA replication stress signature.

Dataset and Inputs: The study included 905 patients across five prostate cancer datasets. The researchers mainly used gene-expression data and also examined somatic mutations, copy-number alterations (CNAs), clinical information, and drug-sensitivity data.

Methods: The researchers selected relevant genes using statistical methods and the Boruta feature-selection algorithm. They compared seven machine-learning algorithms using nested cross-validation to identify the best-performing model.

Main Findings: The XGBoost survival model performed best among the evaluated models. The resulting replication stress signature predicted prostate cancer recurrence across multiple patient cohorts and was associated with clinical and molecular characteristics of the disease.

Relevance to Our Project: This paper provides an example of comparing multiple machine-learning models and evaluating their performance across different datasets. It supports our plan to compare models using somatic mutations, CNAs, and combined genomic features.

Limitation: The study predicted recurrence using mainly gene-expression data. It did not directly classify Stage 1–3 versus Stage 4 using only somatic mutations and CNAs. Therefore, its prediction target and input features differ from our project.

Reference: Huang RH, Hong YK, Du H, et al. A machine learning framework develops a DNA replication stress model for predicting clinical outcomes and therapeutic vulnerability in primary prostate cancer. Journal of Translational Medicine. 2023;21:20. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12967-023-03872-7



Article 4: ATHENA: A Deep Learning-Based AI for Functional Prediction of Genomic Mutations and Synergistic Vulnerabilities in Prostate Cancer

Authors: Cheng et al.
Publication: bioRxiv preprint, 2025
Link: https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.11.26.690813

Objective

The researchers developed ATHENA (Attention-based Therapeutic Network Analyzer), a deep-learning framework designed to identify functionally important genomic mutations and understand how combinations of mutations may contribute to prostate cancer progression and treatment resistance.

Dataset and Inputs

The framework integrated genomic mutation information with RNA and DNA evidence. The researchers used data from multiple cohorts and prostate cancer progression models, including models representing castration-resistant and metastatic disease.

Methods

ATHENA uses an attention-based deep-learning architecture to model nonlinear relationships between mutations. The researchers also used SHAP analysis to interpret the contributions of individual mutations and combinations of mutations to model predictions.

Main Findings

The study reported that ATHENA could identify mutation patterns associated with different prostate cancer disease states and clinical outcomes. It also identified potential interactions between mutations that may contribute to tumour progression and treatment resistance.

Relevance to Our Project

This paper is relevant because it investigates genomic mutations and applies deep learning to prostate cancer. It provides an example of how genomic features and model interpretability can be studied. It also supports the broader rationale for examining mutation-based features in our project.

Limitation

The study focuses on predicting mutation function, mutation interactions, and clinical outcomes rather than directly classifying Stage 1–3 versus Stage 4 using mutation-only, CNA-only, and combined features. In addition, this article is a preprint and has not been peer-reviewed, so its findings should be interpreted cautiously.

Reference

Cheng S, et al. ATHENA: A deep learning-based AI for functional prediction of genomic mutations and synergistic vulnerabilities in prostate cancer. bioRxiv. 2025. https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.11.26.690813



Article 5: Artificial Intelligence Design for Race-Based Prostate Cancer Stage Classification With Multilayer Perceptron: Feature Selection Optimization Approach

Authors: Mulia et al.
Journal: JMIR Formative Research, 2026
Link: https://doi.org/10.2196/82587

Objective

The researchers aimed to develop a machine-learning model to classify prostate cancer into early stages (Stages I–II) and late stages (Stages III–IV). They also investigated whether model performance differed across racial groups.

Dataset and Inputs

The study used publicly available prostate cancer data from the TCGA-PRAD (The Cancer Genome Atlas Prostate Adenocarcinoma) cohort. The researchers combined clinical phenotype information with DNA methylation data. After matching the datasets, 415 samples from White patients were used to develop the main model.

Methods

The researchers identified DNA methylation sites associated with cancer stage using differentially methylated position analysis. They then applied recursive feature elimination (RFE) to select informative features and trained a multilayer perceptron (MLP) model. SHAP and LIME were used to interpret the model's predictions.

Main Findings

The best-performing model achieved 95% accuracy and an area under the ROC curve (AUC) of up to 99% in the majority White cohort using 90 selected features. However, performance declined substantially when the model was evaluated in racial minority groups, highlighting limitations in generalizability.

Relevance to Our Project

This paper is directly relevant because it uses machine learning to classify prostate cancer stages. It provides an example of binary stage classification, feature selection, model evaluation, and model interpretability. These approaches can inform our comparison of somatic mutation-only, CNA-only, and combined mutation+CNA models.

Limitation

The study used DNA methylation and clinical phenotype data rather than somatic mutations and CNAs. Its classification target was Stages I–II versus Stages III–IV, which differs from our proposed classification of Stages 1–3 versus Stage 4. The reported high performance in the majority cohort also did not generalize well to racial minority groups.

Reference

Mulia A, Agustriawan D, Overbeek M, et al. Artificial Intelligence Design for Race-Based Prostate Cancer Stage Classification With Multilayer Perceptron: Feature Selection Optimization Approach. JMIR Formative Research. 2026;10. https://doi.org/10.2196/82587

Article 6: Microsatellite Instability, Tumor Mutational Burden, and Response to Immune Checkpoint Blockade in Patients with Prostate Cancer

Authors: Lenis et al.
Journal: Clinical Cancer Research, 2024
Link: https://doi.org/10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-23-3403

Objective
The authors characterized hypermutated and high-mutational-burden tumors across the MSK prostate cancer cohort to evaluate their clinical presentation and immunotherapy response.

Dataset and Inputs
The study analyzed 3,244 tumor samples across 2,257 prostate cancer patients sequenced with the MSK-IMPACT targeted capture panel, covering between 468 and 505 cancer-related genes.

Methods
The researchers quantified tumor mutational burden and microsatellite instability scores from targeted sequencing data, comparing survival and treatment outcomes across genomic subtypes.

Main Findings
Only about 4.3% of prostate cancer patients showed hypermutated features like MSI-High or high mutational burden. For the remaining 95% of the cohort, mutation rates were low (under 4 mutations per megabase), and point mutation frequencies were very similar between localized tumors and metastatic cases.

Relevance to Our Project
This is the primary study describing our exact cBioPortal dataset (prostate_msk_2024). It provides our biological justification for why point mutations alone are too sparse to separate Stage 4 from earlier stages, and why incorporating copy number alterations is necessary.

Note
The paper focuses on immunotherapy outcomes, whereas our project uses the baseline genomic alterations from the cohort for clinical stage classification.

Reference
Lenis AT, Ravichandran V, Brown S, et al. Microsatellite instability, tumor mutational burden, and response to immune checkpoint blockade in patients with prostate cancer. Clinical Cancer Research. 2024;30(17):3894–3903. https://doi.org/10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-23-3403

Article 7: Genomic Correlates of Clinical Outcome in Advanced Prostate Cancer

Authors: Abida et al.
Journal: Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences (PNAS), 2019
Link: https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1902651116

Objective
The researchers investigated the genomic alterations that define advanced, metastatic prostate cancer and evaluated how specific gene losses impact disease progression.

Dataset and Inputs
The cohort included 444 metastatic tumor samples from 429 patients with castration-resistant prostate cancer, profiled using targeted and whole-exome sequencing.

Methods
The study tracked alteration frequencies across common oncogenic pathways and used survival analysis to correlate individual gene deletions and co-deletions with clinical resistance and survival times.

Main Findings
Advanced metastatic disease was characterized by frequent structural deletions in key tumor suppressors, including TP53 in 53%, PTEN in 32%, and RB1 in 21% of cases, along with AR amplifications in 63%. Combined homozygous deletions across multiple tumor suppressors, particularly PTEN and RB1 together, identified an aggressive subset with very poor outcomes.

Relevance to Our Project
This paper explains the biology behind our copy number features. It shows that Stage 4 disease is driven by the physical deletion of tumor suppressors rather than extra point mutations, supporting our plan to test tree models that can capture combinations of gene losses.

Limitation
The dataset focused on metastatic castration-resistant disease rather than untreated Stage 4 disease at initial diagnosis.

Reference
Abida W, Cyrta J, Heller G, et al. Genomic correlates of clinical outcome in advanced prostate cancer. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences. 2019;116(23):11428–11436. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1902651116

Article 8: Machine Learning for Genetics-Based Classification and Treatment Response Prediction in Cancer of Unknown Primary

Authors: Moon et al.
Journal: Nature Medicine, 2023
Link: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-023-02482-6

Objective
The authors developed a machine learning framework called OncoNPC to predict primary tumor sites and metastatic phenotypes using targeted panel sequencing.

Dataset and Inputs
The study trained on 36,445 tumor samples from cancer centers using MSK-IMPACT and OncoPanel datasets, combining point mutations and discrete copy number calls across 300 to 505 genes.

Methods
The researchers trained an extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost) classifier using nested cross-validation and ran feature ablation experiments to measure the relative value of mutations versus copy number features.

Main Findings
The model achieved an overall AUROC of 0.942 across tumor types, with high precision and recall in prostate cancer. Ablation experiments showed that adding copy number alterations was essential for accurate classification, and gradient-boosted trees handled the sparse tabular panel data without overfitting.

Relevance to Our Project
This study proves that tree-based algorithms work well on targeted panel sequencing like MSK-IMPACT. It supports our plan to combine mutation flags with CNA calls in a tabular format.

Important Difference
The model predicted multiclass cancer types rather than binary disease stages within prostate cancer.

Reference
Moon I, LoPiccolo J, Baca SC, et al. Machine learning for genetics-based classification and treatment response prediction in cancer of unknown primary. Nature Medicine. 2023;29(8):2057–2067. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-023-02482-6

Article 9: Aneuploidy Drives Lethal Progression in Prostate Cancer

Authors: Stopsack et al.
Journal: Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences (PNAS), 2019
Link: https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1902645116

Objective
The authors evaluated whether overall copy number alteration burden and chromosome arm-level aneuploidy predict lethal metastatic progression in men with prostate cancer.

Dataset and Inputs
The study analyzed primary prostate cancer specimens from long-term prospective cohorts, measuring the number of altered chromosome arms and the Fraction of Genome Altered (FGA).

Methods
Cox proportional hazards models and survival analyses were used to test whether arm-level copy number alterations predicted metastasis and death, adjusting for standard clinical variables like PSA and Gleason score.

Main Findings
Total copy number alteration burden independently predicted lethal progression. Men with tumors in the highest quartile of chromosome arm alterations had a five-fold higher risk of metastatic death compared to those with stable genomes.

Relevance to Our Project
This paper reinforces why copy number alterations are a stronger predictor of metastatic Stage 4 disease than point mutations. It shows that chromosomal instability directly correlates with aggressive spread.

Limitation
The study tracked long-term progression over time rather than evaluating a direct cross-sectional stage classification task.

Reference
Stopsack KH, Whittaker CA, Gerke TA, et al. Aneuploidy drives lethal progression in prostate cancer. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences. 2019;116(23):11390–11395. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1902645116

Article 10: CPEM: Accurate Cancer Type Classification Based on Somatic Alterations Using an Ensemble of a Random Forest and a Deep Neural Network

Authors: Lee et al.
Journal: Scientific Reports, 2019
Link: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-53092-8

Objective
The researchers compared the classification performance of somatic point mutations against copy number alterations to determine how much combining both modalities improves model accuracy.

Dataset and Inputs
The study evaluated genomic profiles from 31 cancer types in TCGA, building separate input matrices for somatic mutations alone, copy number alterations alone, and a combined feature set.

Methods
The authors evaluated Random Forests, deep neural networks, and ensemble models across each feature representation and compared performance metrics across datasets.

Main Findings
Models trained solely on point mutations achieved 58.5% classification accuracy. Adding copy number alterations boosted accuracy to 72.7%, representing a 14.2% absolute gain, while an ensemble of Random Forests and deep neural networks reached 84.1%.

Relevance to Our Project
This paper mirrors our exact project setup: comparing mutation-only, CNA-only, and combined models. It provides published evidence that mutations alone are often insufficient, and that copy number data fills in critical predictive signal.

Important Difference
The study focused on pan-cancer tissue-of-origin classification rather than prostate cancer staging.

Reference
Lee K, Park Y, Choi HJ, Lee D. CPEM: accurate cancer type classification based on somatic alterations using an ensemble of a random forest and a deep neural network. Scientific Reports. 2019;9:16584. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-53092-8

Article 11: Why Do Tree-Based Models Still Outperform Deep Learning on Tabular Data?

Authors: Grinsztajn et al.
Journal: Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS), 2022
Link: https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.08815

Objective
The authors ran a large empirical benchmark to evaluate why tree-based models like Random Forest and XGBoost consistently outperform deep learning architectures on tabular datasets.

Dataset and Inputs
The benchmark evaluated 45 open tabular datasets spanning different sample sizes, feature distributions, and tasks.

Methods
The authors systematically compared tree ensembles against neural architectures including Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs), TabNet, and FT-Transformers under standardized hyperparameter tuning.

Main Findings
Tree-based models consistently outperformed deep learning architectures on tabular data. The authors showed that neural networks have inductive biases suited for smooth functions, making them struggle with the irregular decision boundaries and uninformative features that commonly occur in tabular data.

Relevance to Our Project
This benchmark directly supports our model design choices for Section 5.1 of the course specification. It gives us a solid, published rationale for why our classical tree models might perform as well as or better than a neural network on our tabular genomic matrices, which the course rules explicitly recognize as a valid finding.

Reference
Grinsztajn L, Oyallon E, Varoquaux G. Why do tree-based models still outperform deep learning on tabular data? Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems. 2022;35:507–520. https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.08815


Article 1: Biologically Informed Deep Neural Network for Prostate Cancer Discovery
Publication: Nature, 2021. 



Objective
The study aimed to develop a biologically informed deep-learning model to identify molecular features associated with aggressive prostate cancer and treatment resistance. The researchers developed P-NET, a neural network designed to incorporate biological knowledge into its architecture. Unlike a conventional black-box model, P-NET was also designed to help identify molecular pathways and genomic alterations that contribute to its predictions.
Dataset and Inputs
The researchers used publicly available prostate cancer genomic datasets, including information on somatic mutations and copy-number alterations (CNAs). The study examined primary prostate cancer and castration-resistant prostate cancer to investigate molecular differences associated with disease progression and treatment resistance.
Methods
The researchers developed P-NET using a neural-network architecture informed by biological pathways. The model was designed to predict treatment-resistance states and identify important molecular drivers. The researchers also investigated model interpretability to understand which genes and pathways contributed to the predictions.
Main Findings
The study demonstrated how biologically informed deep learning could be used to investigate prostate cancer genomic data and identify molecular features associated with treatment resistance. It also illustrated how an interpretable model can provide biological insights in addition to predictive results.
Relevance to Our Project
This paper is relevant because it demonstrates the potential value of genomic features in prostate cancer modelling. It supports investigating somatic mutations and CNAs as predictive inputs and highlights the importance of model interpretability. Its approach also provides a useful example of how biological knowledge can guide machine-learning development.
Limitation
The study focused on treatment-resistance states and molecular drivers rather than directly classifying Stage 1–3 versus Stage 4 disease. Its biological neural-network architecture is also more complex than the baseline models that may be appropriate for our project. Therefore, its findings motivate our research but do not establish which feature set will perform best for our classification task.
Reference
Elmarakeby HA, et al. Biologically informed deep neural network for prostate cancer discovery. Nature. 2021;598:348–352. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-03922-4


Article 2: A DNA Copy Number Alteration Classifier as a Prognostic Tool for Prostate Cancer Patients
Publication: British Journal of Cancer, 2023.



Objective

The researchers aimed to develop a classifier based on DNA copy-number alterations to predict biochemical recurrence in prostate cancer patients. Biochemical recurrence refers to a rise in prostate-specific antigen (PSA) levels after treatment that may indicate the return of cancer activity. The study addressed the need for improved methods to distinguish patients with relatively indolent disease from those at greater risk of recurrence.

Dataset and Inputs

The study examined 448 prostate cancer tumours from patients with low- or intermediate-risk disease who underwent radical prostatectomy. The researchers assessed CNAs in 14 genes using multiplex ligation-dependent probe amplification (MLPA), a laboratory technique used to measure changes in DNA copy number.

Methods

The researchers used Random Survival Forest for feature selection and Cox proportional hazards regression to develop a six-gene CNA classifier. They assessed the association between the classifier and biochemical recurrence while accounting for established clinicopathological factors. They also evaluated the classifier in independent patient cohorts.

Main Findings

The six-gene classifier was significantly associated with biochemical recurrence, including after adjustment for standard clinicopathological variables. The study reported that the CNA-based score provided additional prognostic information beyond established risk indicators. External validation supported further investigation of CNA-based prognostic tools.

Relevance to Our Project

This paper directly supports investigating CNAs as a separate feature set in our project. It shows that selected copy-number changes can contain clinically relevant information about prostate cancer outcomes. Comparing a CNA-only model with a mutation-only model and a combined model could help determine whether CNAs provide useful predictive information for our specific classification task.

Limitation

The study predicted biochemical recurrence rather than cancer stage. Its patients also had low- or intermediate-risk disease, so its results cannot be directly transferred to a Stage 1–3 versus Stage 4 classification problem. The study supports the potential value of CNAs but does not prove that they will be the strongest predictors of advanced-stage disease.

Reference

Ebrahimizadeh W, et al. A DNA copy number alteration classifier as a prognostic tool for prostate cancer patients. British Journal of Cancer. 2023;128:2165–2174. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41416-023-02236-8



Article 3: A Machine Learning Framework Develops a DNA Replication Stress Model for Predicting Clinical Outcomes and Therapeutic Vulnerability in Primary Prostate Cancer
Publication: Journal of Translational Medicine, 2023.


Objective

The researchers aimed to develop a machine-learning framework to investigate DNA replication stress in primary prostate cancer and its association with clinical outcomes and potential therapeutic vulnerabilities. DNA replication stress occurs when the process of copying DNA is disrupted, which can contribute to genomic instability and cancer progression.

Dataset and Inputs

The study used prostate cancer patient cohorts to examine replication-stress-related molecular features and clinical outcomes. The analysis included multiple cohorts to investigate whether the developed signature could provide useful information beyond a single dataset. The molecular inputs were primarily related to gene expression and DNA replication-stress biology rather than the direct mutation and CNA feature sets proposed for our project.

Methods

The researchers applied feature-selection procedures and compared multiple machine-learning algorithms to develop a replication-stress signature. They evaluated model performance using cross-validation and independent cohorts. The study also examined the relationships between the resulting signature, clinical outcomes, and potential treatment vulnerabilities.

Main Findings

The study reported that its replication-stress signature was associated with prostate cancer clinical outcomes, including biochemical recurrence. Its use of multiple cohorts provided evidence about the reproducibility of the findings across datasets. The analysis also suggested that replication-stress biology may help identify differences in tumour behaviour and potential therapeutic vulnerabilities.

Relevance to Our Project

This paper is useful for understanding how molecular features can be translated into predictive models. It highlights the importance of comparing candidate algorithms, selecting features carefully, and validating performance across independent datasets. These principles are relevant to our plan to compare mutation-only, CNA-only, and combined genomic models.

Limitation

The study investigated replication-stress-related molecular features and clinical outcomes rather than directly classifying Stage 1–3 versus Stage 4 using somatic mutations and CNAs. Therefore, its findings support the general modelling strategy but do not directly establish the predictive value of our proposed feature sets.

Reference

Huang RH, et al. A machine learning framework develops a DNA replication stress model for predicting clinical outcomes and therapeutic vulnerability in primary prostate cancer. Journal of Translational Medicine. 2023. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9835390/



Article 4: ATHENA — A Deep Learning-Based AI for Functional Prediction of Genomic Mutations and Synergistic Vulnerabilities in Prostate Cancer



Objective

The study introduced ATHENA, a deep-learning framework designed to investigate the functional effects of genomic mutations in prostate cancer. Its broader objective was to identify mutations and combinations of mutations that may contribute to disease progression, treatment resistance, and potential therapeutic vulnerabilities.

Dataset and Inputs

The framework examines genomic mutation information alongside additional molecular evidence, including DNA- and RNA-related information. Rather than treating each mutation as an isolated feature, the approach investigates relationships between mutations and their possible biological effects in prostate cancer.

Methods

ATHENA uses an attention-based deep-learning approach to investigate complex relationships among genomic features. The researchers also describe interpretability methods, including SHAP, to investigate which features contribute to model predictions. This approach aims to provide insight into the potential functional importance of individual mutations and mutation combinations.

Main Findings

The work presents a framework for investigating mutation function and interactions in prostate cancer. It suggests that considering relationships between genomic alterations may provide information that is not captured by examining individual mutations alone. However, the reported findings should be interpreted cautiously because the work was initially published as a preprint and requires assessment of its peer-review status and independent validation.

Relevance to Our Project

This paper is relevant to our mutation-based analysis because it illustrates how genomic alterations can be investigated using machine learning. It also raises the possibility that interactions among genomic features may be informative. Our project can test a simpler, more directly interpretable question first: whether mutation-only, CNA-only, or combined features best distinguish Stage 1–3 from Stage 4 in our selected cohort.

Limitation

The study focuses on mutation function, interactions, and potential therapeutic vulnerabilities rather than the exact stage-classification task proposed in our project. Its deep-learning architecture may also be more complex than necessary for a small or moderately sized dataset. In addition, preprint findings should not be treated as equivalent to independently peer-reviewed evidence.

Reference

ATHENA: A deep learning-based AI for functional prediction of genomic mutations and synergistic vulnerabilities in prostate cancer. Preprint. 2025. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12676378/



Article 5: Artificial Intelligence Design for Race-Based Prostate Cancer Stage Classification With Multilayer Perceptron: Feature Selection Optimization Approach
Publication: JMIR Formative Research, 2026.


Objective

The researchers aimed to develop a machine-learning model to classify prostate cancer into early stages (Stages I–II) and late stages (Stages III–IV). They also investigated whether the model could generalize across racial groups. This is particularly important because genomic and molecular datasets may contain unequal representation of different populations.

Dataset and Inputs

The study used DNA methylation and clinical phenotype information from The Cancer Genome Atlas Prostate Adenocarcinoma (TCGA-PRAD) dataset. The researchers identified methylation features associated with cancer stage and developed their main model using a majority-White patient cohort. They subsequently examined how the model performed in other racial groups.

Methods

The researchers used differentially methylated position analysis to identify relevant DNA methylation sites and recursive feature elimination to select a smaller set of features. These features were used to train a multilayer perceptron (MLP) classifier. SHAP and LIME were applied to interpret the model's predictions and investigate the contribution of individual features.

Main Findings

The best-performing model achieved 95% accuracy and an area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) of up to 0.99 in the majority-White cohort using 90 selected features. However, performance declined substantially when the model was applied to racial minority groups. These findings highlight that strong performance in one population does not necessarily guarantee reliable predictions in another.

Relevance to Our Project

This paper is especially relevant because it directly investigates prostate cancer stage classification using machine learning. It provides an example of feature selection, model evaluation, and interpretation. It also demonstrates the importance of checking generalizability rather than relying only on high accuracy in the primary dataset.

Limitation

The study used DNA methylation rather than somatic mutations or CNAs. It classified Stages I–II versus Stages III–IV, whereas our project proposes Stages 1–3 versus Stage 4. Its results therefore cannot directly establish the performance expected from our model. The observed reduction in performance across racial groups also emphasizes the need to evaluate the representativeness and limitations of our own cohort.

Reference

Mulia A, Agustriawan D, Overbeek M, Widjaja M, Kurniawan V, Syechlo J, Ahmad MI, Sathipati SY, Kurubanjerdjit N. Artificial Intelligence Design for Race-Based Prostate Cancer Stage Classification With Multilayer Perceptron: Feature Selection Optimization Approach. JMIR Formative Research. 2026;10. https://doi.org/10.2196/82587
